Vincent's own usage in Notices et extraits 16.2 is decisive for the meaning of the brackets. In that volume [ ] always encloses words not in the witness, supplied by the editor. He says so in his notes:
- p. 374 n. 6: 'Au lieu de cette parenthèse, il n'y a dans le ms. que l'article τῶν';
- p. 405 n. 1: 'Tous les mss. omettent cette répétition';
- p. 425 n. 2: 'Ces dix mots manquent dans notre texte';
- p. 461 n. 12: '[τῆς]' with 'Mss. om. τῆς';
- p. 478 n. 6: 'Mss. om.'

The 1693 Paris edition confirms the practice independently: Vincent's unannotated [καὶ δὶς ὅταν τὸ κ,] and [ὅταν] are absent from his source.

His sign for deletion is different: round brackets with 'Mot surabondant', as in (εἰς) p. 292, (καὶ) p. 418, (τὴν) p. 425. In single-witness texts he supplies articles in [ ] without a note: [τῶν] p. 242, [τῷ] p. 248.

So P had no article before δίεσις, and option a is excluded. Nothing decides between b and c. That is an editorial choice between Vincent's supplement and P's text without the article, and both are defensible Greek.
